# graphtask environment.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and adjust. `npm start` runs node with
# `--env-file=.env`, so any variable set here is in the process environment.

# ---- Database ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Either set DATABASE_URL directly...
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/graphtask
#
# ...or set the bootstrap URL + DB name and we'll splice them together at
# runtime (this is what the Wafer environment uses).
PG_BOOTSTRAP_URL=postgresql://postgres@localhost/postgres
DATABASE_NAME=graphtask

# Port the Express server binds to on 127.0.0.1. Optional; default 3000.
# PORT=3000

# ---- Uploads (optional) -----------------------------------------------------
# Node background images (and pasted images in the editor) are POSTed to
# /api/graphs/:gid/uploads and stored as BYTEA inside the project's Postgres
# database. No separate object store, no extension required.
#
# Per-upload byte cap. Default 5 MB. Raise it for users who routinely paste
# large diagrams; lower it to keep the uploads table small.
# GRAPHTASK_UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=5242880
#
# Cleanup: a graph DELETE cascades to its uploads. Replacing or removing a
# node's background-image does NOT yet delete the old row — those orphans
# stay until the graph itself is deleted. See the roadmap entry on the
# upload orphan reaper if storage growth becomes a concern.

# ---- Semantic search (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# Tier-1 dense search (#173 §9): OFF by default — lexical search always works.
# Configuring an embedding backend turns on (a) the write-path indexer that
# keeps the pgvector `task_chunks` store in step with your nodes, and (b) the
# dense leg of every search, fused with lexical via RRF.
#
# EMBEDDING_BACKEND=none        # default: dense off
# EMBEDDING_BACKEND=local-onnx  # in-process CPU model, zero extra services.
#                               # Needs the optional dep:
#                               #   npm install @huggingface/transformers
# EMBEDDING_BACKEND=http        # your own TEI server or a Modal endpoint;
#                               # set EMBEDDING_URL (+ EMBEDDING_TOKEN or
#                               # MODAL_KEY/MODAL_SECRET) and EMBEDDING_MODEL.
#
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=Xenova/bge-small-en-v1.5   # local-onnx default (384-dim)
# EMBEDDING_DIM=384                          # usually learned automatically
#
# The vector store needs pgvector >= 0.8.2 in Postgres. Without it, dense
# search still runs — ranked in-memory per query (slower on big graphs).
#
# ---- Rerank (Tier 2, optional, OFF by default) ------------------------------
# Cross-encoder that reorders the top fused hits for precision (best single
# answer to the top). Recall-first flows don't need it; the agent / "one best
# result" flow does. The #198 bake-off picked a small model that runs on CPU.
#
# RERANK_BACKEND=local-onnx     # in-process CPU cross-encoder, no GPU
# RERANK_MODEL=Xenova/ms-marco-TinyBERT-L-2-v2  # local default; MiniLM-L-2 is a
#                               # slightly-stronger, ~4x slower fallback (#198)
# RERANK_DTYPE=q8               # int8: same accuracy as fp32, faster (default q8)
# RERANK_MAXCHARS=512           # doc text cap. Seq length dominates latency;
#                               # 512 ≈ 62ms/20docs on 1 core, accuracy held.
#                               # Raise if your signal sits deep in long bodies.
# RERANK_TOPM=50                # rerank this many fused hits (default 50 = the
#                               # whole fused list). Top-20 strands relevant docs
#                               # in positions 21-50; top-50 lifts them (recall@20
#                               # 0.871->0.892) at ~same cost — so 50 > 20 (#198).
# RERANK_BACKEND=http           # GPU/Modal track for the big bge-reranker-v2-m3;
#                               # set RERANK_URL (+ auth) and RERANK_MODEL.

# ---- Auth (optional) --------------------------------------------------------
# graphtask runs with NO AUTH by default. Every graph id is a 16-char random
# string and is bearer-token equivalent: anyone with the URL can read/edit.
# This is the right model for local dev, self-hosted single-user installs, and
# anyone happy with link-shared graphs.
#
# Hosted / multi-tenant deployments can opt into auth by setting:
AUTH_PROVIDER=none
#   none   — default, no sign-in, no ownership, no ACL.
#   clerk  — Clerk-issued sessions; new graphs created by signed-in users get
#            an owner_user_id. Legacy un-owned graphs keep URL-bearer access.
#
# For AUTH_PROVIDER=clerk, the following are REQUIRED at boot:
# CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
#
# Get keys from https://dashboard.clerk.com (Email OTP is the only sign-in
# method graphtask wires up; you can leave the rest off in your Clerk app).
